==French==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|fr}}, from {{etyl|fro|fr}} {{term|donjon|lang=fro}}, {{term|dongon||castle keep|lang=fro}}, of {{etyl|gem|fr}} origin, from {{etyl|frk|fr}} *{{term||dungjo, *dunjon-|dungeon, bower, underground cellar|lang=frk}}, from {{proto|Germanic|dungijō||dungijōn||dungō|enclosed space, vault, bower, treasury|lang=fr}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|dhengh-|to cover|lang=fr}}. Cognate with {{ang}} {{term|dung||prison, dungeon|lang=ang}}, {{osx}} {{term|dung||underground cellar|lang=osx}}, {{goh}} {{term|tung||underground cellar|lang=goh}}, {{non}} {{term|dyngja||a lady's bower|lang=non}}. More at [[dung]].

Alternate etymology traces Old French ''donjon'', from Vulgar Latin ''*dominio'' ‘lord's castle’, from Latin [[dominus]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/dɔ̃ʒɔ̃/|lang=fr}}

===Noun===
